{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CghAQPpdEpeWPHVaEiA1Z", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**BillingService** and **PaymentsService** are the two services that would lose message delivery. They are the only services that call the NotificationsAPI directly.\n\nHow they depend on the Redis instance, according to the graph:\n\n1. **NotificationsService** stores its data in **NotificationsRedisInstance**. The graph shows no other dependency for it, so losing that Redis instance takes out its only listed backing store.\n2. **NotificationsService** exposes the **NotificationsAPI**.\n3. **BillingService** and **PaymentsService** both call **NotificationsAPI** directly, one hop away. No other service does.\n\nThe only configuration key on NotificationsService is **RETRY_ATTEMPTS**: three attempts with a 250ms backoff. Those retries won't fix a lost Redis instance, so requests from BillingService and PaymentsService would fail once the three attempts are used up.\n\nThe graph doesn't say whether NotificationsService can fall back to anything without Redis, such as an in-memory buffer or a second store. So it's an inference that delivery stops completely, based on there being no other dependency listed. I only looked at direct callers. Services that call BillingService or PaymentsService could also be affected indirectly."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 267, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5158, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 267}, "output_tokens": 396, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a73dd72f-0031-4358-bb81-485a7ef11d6f", "uuid": "760b7026-79af-4875-82b4-b8f0b86f8996"